  const pages = {
    overview: `
      <h2>Overview</h2>

      <p>After finishing <em>Good For Me</em>, I felt ready to make a bigger mod, an incumbent simulator that explored a scenario few people had touched. Ever since Donald Trump won the 2024 election, some people have wondered what might have happened if the big orange star had won a second consecutive term, and whether that would have been better or worse for the United States and the world in the long run. Few mods had explored the idea, so I decided to take it on. I can now proudly say the project is finished.</p>

      <p>Its original title was <em>The Nail</em>, after the famous proverb “For Want of a Nail,” but I realized it was too generic and changed it to <em>The Heir</em>. I wanted to tell the story of the American right, its many factions and internal feuds, and the fascinating figure who leads it today. I also wanted to make a fun, replayable game packed with the little details and Easter eggs I’ve always loved finding in video games. Whether I pulled it off is up to you and everyone else who plays it.</p>

      <p>They say that to take a genuine interest in a subject, you need to have at least some empathy for it. I can’t deny that, while writing this game, I tended to sympathize and identify with figures closer to conservatism, though my sympathies rarely extended to populism or the identitarian right. I tried to be fair and even-handed, but I know my politics seep into the dialogue and advisor feedback. I also tried to keep from taking the whole thing too seriously by giving the writing a streak of dark humor. That rarely hurts when you’re dealing with politics and history.</p>

      <p>Finally, thank you, <span data-heir-reading-assistant></span>, for playing, though I have no idea if that’s your real name, haha. Did you enjoy it? If so, I’m glad. If you didn’t, I’m glad anyway! At least you finished it. If you have any criticism or suggestions, drop me a message on Reddit or Discord. And once again, because I can’t say it enough, thank you for playing my game. See you next time.</p>

      <p>— Trystant</p>
    `,

    sources: `
      <h2>Sources</h2>

      <p>One nice thing about making a game set in the recent past is that you can get a lot of the information you need from memory and by following the news. Even so, I read several books while writing <em>The Heir</em>, and I’d recommend them to anyone who wants to dig deeper into the world of the mod.</p>

      <div class="heir-reading-book">
        <img class="heir-reading-cover heir-reading-cover-right"
          src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/c/c5/Confidence_Man_cover.jpg"
          alt="Cover of Confidence Man by Maggie Haberman" loading="lazy">
        <p>If Trump had won the 2020 election, his cabinet would probably have looked more like the one from his first term, with plenty of establishment figures and members of the Republican old guard. That’s the world Maggie Haberman describes in her Trump biography, <a href="https://www.amazon.com/Confidence-Man-Making-Breaking-America/dp/0593297342"><cite>Confidence Man: The Making of Donald Trump and the Breaking of America</cite></a>. It’s a great book, full of detail about the 45th president. Haberman had been covering Trump since before 2016 and had exceptional access to the inner workings of his first administration. It’s a perfect way into the wild, narcissistic, vain mind that has been steering the fate of the new Rome for some time now. I had a great time reading it.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="heir-reading-book">
        <img class="heir-reading-cover heir-reading-cover-left"
          src="https://i.imgur.com/Q2DtDwh.jpeg"
          alt="Cover of Por qué se rompió Estados Unidos by Roger Senserrich" loading="lazy">
        <p>But there’s more driving America than Trump. To understand how he reached the presidency and why the United States is in crisis, I read political scientist Roger Senserrich’s <a href="https://www.amazon.es/dp/8419642630"><cite>Por qué se rompió Estados Unidos: Populismo y polarización en la era Trump</cite></a> (<em>Why America Broke: Populism and Polarization in the Trump Era</em>). Most of you probably won’t read it, but Senserrich is a fellow Spaniard who has lived in the United States for decades. He’s a member of the Working Families Party, is close to Senator Ned Lamont, and makes no secret of his progressive leanings. The book gives a good overview of America’s current problems and why Trump came to power.</p>
      </div>

      <p>Some of you may also have noticed the comparisons the game draws between Donald Trump’s presidency and Adolf Hitler’s rise to power. I don’t think Trump is literally Hitler, but I see parallels between the MAGA movement and the nationalist, authoritarian right of the 1930s. If you’d like to read more on that subject, I recommend Roger Griffin’s <a href="https://www.amazon.es/dp/1949845125"><cite>Fascism: A Quick Immersion</cite></a>. For a deeper look, try Ian Kershaw’s <a href="https://www.amazon.es/dp/0141035889"><cite>Hitler</cite></a> and Sebastian Haffner’s <a href="https://www.amazon.es/dp/B00AEDPNPA"><cite>Jekyll and Hyde: An Eyewitness Analysis of Nazi Germany</cite></a>.</p>

      <p>For the foreign policy side of the mod, I also did some reading on the conflicts in Ukraine and Palestine, especially the latter. Here are a few books I’d recommend to anyone interested:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://www.amazon.es/dp/009954203X"><cite>Postwar</cite></a>, by Tony Judt.</li>
        <li><a href="https://www.amazon.es/dp/0300257074"><cite>Homelands: A Personal History of Europe</cite></a>, by Timothy Garton Ash.</li>
        <li><a href="https://www.amazon.es/dp/1324051191"><cite>The Russo-Ukrainian War: The Return of History</cite></a>, by Serhii Plokhy.</li>
        <li><a href="https://www.amazon.es/dp/B09QLB3FP4"><cite>Prophets without Honor</cite></a>, by Shlomo Ben-Ami.</li>
        <li><a href="https://www.amazon.es/dp/0385521707"><cite>My Promised Land: The Triumph and Tragedy of Israel</cite></a>, by Ari Shavit.</li>
